Here's how to save and restore draft text using Window lifecycle events:

**App.xaml.cs** — Override `CreateWindow` and hook `Stopped`/`Resumed`/`Destroying`:

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
    {
        var window = base.CreateWindow(activationState);

        window.Stopped += (s, e) =>
            Preferences.Set("draft_text", GetCurrentDraft());

        window.Resumed += (s, e) =>
            RestoreDraft(Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty));

        // Android back-button can skip Stopped entirely
        window.Destroying += (s, e) =>
            Preferences.Set("draft_text", GetCurrentDraft());

        return window;
    }

    private string GetCurrentDraft()
    {
        if (Windows[0].Page is AppShell shell
            && shell.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
            return page.ViewModel.DraftText;
        return string.Empty;
    }

    private void RestoreDraft(string text)
    {
        if (Windows[0].Page is AppShell shell
            && shell.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
            page.ViewModel.DraftText = text;
    }
}
```

**NotePage ViewModel** — also restore on initial load:

```csharp
public partial class NoteViewModel : ObservableObject
{
    [ObservableProperty]
    private string draftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
}
```

**Key points:**

1. **`Stopped`** fires when the app is fully backgrounded — save here.
2. **`Resumed`** fires when returning from background — restore here.
3. **`Destroying`** is also needed because Android's back button can skip `Stopped`.
4. Use `Preferences` for small strings — it's fast enough to avoid ANR/watchdog kills (keep handlers under 1–2 seconds).
5. `Resumed` does **not** fire on first launch (only `Created` → `Activated`), so initialize from `Preferences` in your ViewModel constructor.
